<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sobornoe Ulozhenie</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1649</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Sobornoye Ulozheniye, Law Code of 1649</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sobornoe Ulozhenie was the law code of the Tsardom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> adopted by an assembly of the land in 1649 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> Alexis, after the Salt Riot of 1648. Its 25 chapters codified criminal, civil and administrative law, abolished time limits on the recovery of runaway peasants, so completing their legal enserfment, and tied townspeople to their communities. It remained the basis of Russian law until the codification of the 1830s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sobornoe-ulozhenie.jpg" alt="Sobornoe Ulozhenie" width="320" height="456" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sobornoe_Ulozhenie.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
